content.text:
The final test should scan the entire top-level `agent` package—not only `capabilities.go`. That is how we avoid missing helpers moved into other files.

I verified current `main` has:

- 23 top-level `As*` capability functions.
- 21 in `capabilities.go`.
- `AsContextInjector` in `inject.go`.
- `AsModelLister` in `model_lister.go`.
- 13 currently ungated and 10 declared.

So the reviewer’s proposed `capabilities.go`-only scan would still miss two existing capabilities.

## Final shape

I’d use two architecture tests sharing one source scanner:

```go
func TestCapabilityHelpersUseStandardResolver(t *testing.T)
func TestDeclaredCapsBuiltinListMatchesCapabilityHelpers(t *testing.T)
```

### 1. Enforce the resolver architecture

Scan every non-test `.go` file in the top-level `agent` package.

For every top-level function named `As*`:

- It must have no receiver.
- Its body must return exactly one resolver call.
- That resolver must be either:
  - `builtinCapability[T]`
  - `declaredCapability[T]`
- Extract `T` as the capability’s canonical name.
- Fail on any other implementation.

Conceptually:

```go
func inspectCapabilityHelper(t *testing.T, fn *ast.FuncDecl) capabilityHelper {
	t.Helper()

	// Require this shape:
	//
	// func AsFoo(...) (...) {
	//     return builtinCapability[Foo](...)
	// }
	//
	// or declaredCapability[Foo](...).

	resolver, capability := resolverCall(t, fn)

	switch resolver {
	case "builtinCapability":
		return capabilityHelper{
			Function:   fn.Name.Name,
			Capability: capability,
			Builtin:    true,
		}
	case "declaredCapability":
		return capabilityHelper{
			Function:   fn.Name.Name,
			Capability: capability,
			Builtin:    false,
		}
	default:
		t.Fatalf(
			"%s must use builtinCapability or declaredCapability",
			fn.Name.Name,
		)
	}
}
```

This catches a future raw assertion immediately:

```go
func AsFoo(ag Agent) (Foo, bool) {
	foo, ok := ag.(Foo)
	return foo, ok
}
```

Failure:

```text
AsFoo must use builtinCapability or declaredCapability
```

### 2. Compare discovered built-ins against the documentation

Make the `DeclaredCaps` comment machine-readable:

```go
// DeclaredCaps enumerates optional capabilities exposed through the external
// agent protocol.
//
// The following capabilities intentionally have no DeclaredCaps gate:
//
//   - ContextInjector
//   - EffectiveHookDiagnostics
//   - HookFreshness
//   - ModelExtractor
//   - ModelLister
//   - SessionBaseDirProvider
//   - SessionEndBudgeter
//   - SidecarImageProvider
//   - SkillEventExtractor
//   - SubagentSessionResolver
//   - ToolInvocationScanner
//   - TranscriptFetcher
//   - TranscriptSanitizer
//
// Their As* helpers resolve through builtinCapability.
```

The second test:

1. Locates the `DeclaredCaps` type declaration.
2. Reads the bullet list from its doc comment.
3. Collects every `T` used by `builtinCapability[T]`.
4. Sorts both lists.
5. Compares them exactly.

Failure should clearly distinguish both directions:

```text
DeclaredCaps built-in exclusion list is out of sync:
missing from documentation: [FooScanner]
documented but not built-in: [OldCapability]
```

That catches both:

- A new built-in capability missing from the comment.
- A capability changed to declared but left in the comment.

## Required normalization

Before enabling the architecture rule, four existing helpers need behavior-preserving normalization:

```go
AsSidecarImageProvider     → builtinCapability
AsStreamingTextGenerator   → declaredCapability
AsContextInjector          → builtinCapability
AsModelLister              → builtinCapability
```

Without this cleanup, the test would need special cases—which defeats the point of establishing a simple architectural vocabulary.

## Why we should not hard-code counts

The test should not assert:

```go
if len(helpers) != 23 { ... }
```

Counts inevitably become another stale inventory. It should discover all `As*` functions dynamically and compare semantic sets. Adding capability number 24 is then supported naturally; the test only asks the author to classify and document it.

## Coverage boundary

The scanner should:

- Scan all production `.go` files directly inside `cmd/entire/cli/agent`.
- Exclude `_test.go`.
- Exclude methods with receivers.
- Exclude subpackages such as `agent/codex` or `agent/external`.
- Treat every top-level `As*` function in that package as a capability resolver.

This boundary includes the two helpers the original proposal would miss while avoiding unrelated `As*` names elsewhere in the repository.

The existing AST infrastructure belongs in [architecture_test.go](/Users/muskanpaliwal/Work/open-source/entire/cli/fix-declared-caps-builtins/cmd/entire/cli/agent/architecture_test.go), with the production normalization in [capabilities.go](/Users/muskanpaliwal/Work/open-source/entire/cli/fix-declared-caps-builtins/cmd/entire/cli/agent/capabilities.go), `inject.go`, and `model_lister.go`.

No usable checkpoint history was available for these definitions, so this design is inferred from the verified current source and Git history.

Here, “normalize” means rewriting several functions that do the same job in different ways so they all use the same standard pattern.

It does not mean changing their behavior.

For example, `AsSidecarImageProvider` currently performs its own type assertion:

```go
func AsSidecarImageProvider(ag Agent) (SidecarImageProvider, bool) {
	if ag == nil {
		return nil, false
	}
	p, ok := ag.(SidecarImageProvider)
	return p, ok
}
```

But the shared helper already performs exactly that work:

```go
func AsSidecarImageProvider(ag Agent) (SidecarImageProvider, bool) {
	return builtinCapability[SidecarImageProvider](ag)
}
```

Same inputs, same outputs, simpler and consistent.

Similarly, `AsStreamingTextGenerator` manually implements declaration gating:

```go
func AsStreamingTextGenerator(ag Agent) (StreamingTextGenerator, bool) {
	if ag == nil {
		return nil, false
	}

	stg, ok := ag.(StreamingTextGenerator)
	if !ok {
		return nil, false
	}

	if cd, ok := ag.(CapabilityDeclarer); ok {
		return stg, cd.DeclaredCapabilities().StreamingTextGenerator
	}

	return stg, true
}
```

It can use the existing standard helper:

```go
func AsStreamingTextGenerator(ag Agent) (StreamingTextGenerator, bool) {
	return declaredCapability[StreamingTextGenerator](
		ag,
		func(c DeclaredCaps) bool {
			return c.StreamingTextGenerator
		},
	)
}
```

Again, the behavior remains the same.

After normalization, every capability resolver has one of two shapes:

```go
return builtinCapability[Foo](ag)
```

or:

```go
return declaredCapability[Foo](ag, declarationCheck)
```

That gives the architecture test a reliable vocabulary:

```text
builtinCapability  → no DeclaredCaps gate
declaredCapability → requires a DeclaredCaps gate
anything else      → test failure
```

The four existing functions needing this cleanup are:

- `AsSidecarImageProvider` → `builtinCapability`
- `AsStreamingTextGenerator` → `declaredCapability`
- `AsContextInjector` → `builtinCapability`
- `AsModelLister` → `builtinCapability`

A simple analogy: four doors currently use different homemade locks. Normalizing means fitting them with one of two standard locks. The doors still open for exactly the same people, but now we can reliably inspect the lock type to know the access rule.

